1
00:00:08,123 --> 00:00:21,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can one... or how should one train oneself to become unaffected by harsh speech or by evil actions done by others towards oneself?

2
00:00:22,668 --> 00:00:23,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Aim at oneself?

3
00:00:23,668 --> 00:00:23,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

4
00:00:25,029 --> 00:00:27,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Like if people are insulting you?

5
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:27,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

6
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:31,831
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you make yourself... Well, the suttas were quite clear on it.

7
00:00:33,452 --> 00:00:33,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You...

8
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:41,341
[SPEAKER_00]: You would endure the harsh speech, the insults, in the same manner you would endure the elements.

9
00:00:42,482 --> 00:00:51,146
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same manner you would endure wind, rain, heat or cold, or bites of insects, you also endure the harsh words.

10
00:00:52,146 --> 00:00:56,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's really a... it's no brainer in a sense of... you need to really figure it out.

11
00:00:56,528 --> 00:01:03,632
[SPEAKER_00]: It just comes down to being able to endure it without resorting to... well, to ill-will yourself.

12
00:01:08,313 --> 00:01:16,116
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason people cannot endure it is because the habit of acting out of it is just too established and they keep maintaining it.

13
00:01:16,977 --> 00:01:36,365
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to break that habit of acting out of ill-will, in order to break the habit of acting out of ill-will, you just need to stop acting out of ill-will and endure that unpleasant feeling that arises when somebody attacks you verbally or... Because no amount of things said at you

14
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:38,278
[SPEAKER_00]: It can actually harm you.

15
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:42,340
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you not being able to keep the mind away from it.

16
00:01:43,301 --> 00:01:46,082
[SPEAKER_00]: You not being able to withstand things that are said at you.

17
00:01:46,522 --> 00:01:48,423
[SPEAKER_00]: You jump into it and that's why you suffer.

18
00:01:48,703 --> 00:01:50,364
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you get affected by it.

19
00:01:53,365 --> 00:02:01,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people get offended, nobody denies the offensive words, but you becoming offended, that's on you.

20
00:02:02,609 --> 00:02:05,490
[SPEAKER_00]: That's on your own inability to keep your mind out of it.

21
00:02:10,282 --> 00:02:28,867
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said in that simile, when he was walking Pindapartha, when that man came in front of a man's house and he started shouting abuse at him, this man, and as the Buddha was walking away, he turned to the man and said, well, let me ask you something.

22
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:32,928
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have guests come to your home and you give them gifts and

23
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:35,078
[SPEAKER_00]: But they don't take them.

24
00:02:35,559 --> 00:02:36,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Who do those gifts stay for?

25
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:38,980
[SPEAKER_00]: And the man says, well, of course, they stay for me.

26
00:02:39,141 --> 00:02:39,761
[SPEAKER_00]: They're my gifts.

27
00:02:39,801 --> 00:02:41,162
[SPEAKER_00]: If they didn't take them, they stay for me.

28
00:02:41,562 --> 00:02:44,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, well, so it's with your insults and your harsh words.

29
00:02:45,064 --> 00:02:45,884
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not taking them.

30
00:02:46,284 --> 00:02:47,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just stays with you.

31
00:02:47,825 --> 00:02:54,168
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not just a poetic kind of poetic put-down.

32
00:02:54,648 --> 00:02:55,149
[SPEAKER_00]: It's genuine.

33
00:02:55,249 --> 00:02:57,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Phenomenologically, that's what happens if the

34
00:02:58,550 --> 00:03:01,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind does not respond with ill-will to ill-will.

35
00:03:02,399 --> 00:03:06,368
[SPEAKER_00]: The only experience with ill-will that's left, is the experience of that man.

36
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:08,834
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's the only one left with anger.

37
00:03:10,418 --> 00:03:20,103
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the Buddha did give in to anger and then replied something or put him in his place out of anger and so on, well, then there would have been an engagement and that would have fueled further and then the man would have not.

38
00:03:20,463 --> 00:03:32,530
[SPEAKER_00]: But he just, by not responding to it, by being able to take it, without being actually affected by it, it highlighted that no amount of abuse can actually step outside of that man's experience.

39
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:36,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was the only one left with that painful anger.

40
00:03:36,412 --> 00:03:36,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody else.

41
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:41,061
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that sutta that you are talking about.

42
00:03:41,201 --> 00:03:42,981
[SPEAKER_01]: I got a little excerpt from it.

43
00:03:43,442 --> 00:03:43,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

44
00:03:43,942 --> 00:03:44,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Go on.

45
00:03:44,222 --> 00:03:45,522
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read it short.

46
00:03:46,383 --> 00:04:01,408
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, the Buddha says, whoever returns insult to one who is insultinging, returns taunt to one who is taunting, returns a berating to one who is berating, is said to be eating together, sharing company with that person.

47
00:04:02,168 --> 00:04:05,649
[SPEAKER_01]: But I am neither eating together nor sharing your company, Brahman.

48
00:04:06,149 --> 00:04:06,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all yours.

49
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:07,550
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all yours.

50
00:04:07,711 --> 00:04:08,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

51
00:04:08,091 --> 00:04:08,872
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what he said.

52
00:04:08,892 --> 00:04:09,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

53
00:04:09,272 --> 00:04:09,872
[SPEAKER_00]: What sutta is that?

54
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:10,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is it?

55
00:04:10,593 --> 00:04:12,835
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have the reference here, but... Ah, okay.

56
00:04:13,615 --> 00:04:13,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

57
00:04:14,356 --> 00:04:15,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the one I was thinking of.

58
00:04:16,018 --> 00:04:16,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

59
00:04:16,858 --> 00:04:18,560
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't remember the rest of it, but...

60
00:04:19,841 --> 00:04:24,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what... it reminds me of another sutta as well, I'll come to, that

61
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:35,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Forbearance, patience with a person who is getting angry at you, that is said to be the foremost good.

62
00:04:36,598 --> 00:04:39,040
[SPEAKER_01]: The foremost good for you, the foremost good for that person.

63
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:46,567
[SPEAKER_01]: But others would say, well, if you just... you don't react, you don't respond to that person,

64
00:04:47,227 --> 00:04:56,252
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't fight back, then that person just... and you just stay patient and calm and act like a weak, weak person in front of that person.

65
00:04:56,452 --> 00:04:56,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

66
00:04:56,792 --> 00:04:58,313
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just going to attack you even more.

67
00:04:58,513 --> 00:04:59,554
[SPEAKER_01]: He's going to just chase you.

68
00:05:00,154 --> 00:05:00,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

69
00:05:00,614 --> 00:05:01,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you should fight back.

70
00:05:01,795 --> 00:05:03,776
[SPEAKER_01]: You should with the stick, basically.

71
00:05:03,876 --> 00:05:09,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that comes out of a, out of a confusion between what weakness is.

72
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:14,162
[SPEAKER_00]: You're weak when you're mentally affected by someone's anger.

73
00:05:15,758 --> 00:05:23,426
[SPEAKER_00]: As there is usually, in practical terms, when somebody is affected mentally, that's when they start acting bodily, to defend themselves.

74
00:05:24,468 --> 00:05:28,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you show your weakness, by being affected mentally.

75
00:05:28,872 --> 00:05:38,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, people who think that, oh, you must do something in return, otherwise you'll be perceived weak, they confuse doing something in return with being affected mentally.

76
00:05:38,842 --> 00:05:43,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're not affected mentally, and you don't do anything in return, the other person will see that.

77
00:05:44,347 --> 00:05:48,732
[SPEAKER_00]: They will see that you are not bullied mentally, despite not fighting back physically.

78
00:05:50,586 --> 00:05:52,168
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't break the mind, literally.

79
00:05:52,208 --> 00:05:52,789
[SPEAKER_00]: That's on you.

80
00:05:53,289 --> 00:05:57,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You give in to that, or not.

81
00:05:58,095 --> 00:06:02,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you cannot retaliate physically without being affected mentally.

82
00:06:03,802 --> 00:06:07,166
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can remain unaffected mentally and not do anything physically.

83
00:06:09,242 --> 00:06:12,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing something physically, in return, requires you to be affected mentally.

84
00:06:13,724 --> 00:06:25,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, in order to not be able to... in order to not be affected mentally, you first need to stop entertaining the habit of doing something physically that requires mental effect as well, underneath.

85
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:31,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you stop acting physically, then you highlight for yourself the fact that the problem is not you.

86
00:06:31,993 --> 00:06:33,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I should have done this, I should have done that.

87
00:06:33,895 --> 00:06:34,035
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

88
00:06:34,875 --> 00:06:37,957
[SPEAKER_00]: All I should have done is not allow myself to be affected mentally.

89
00:06:38,498 --> 00:06:40,559
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whatever you do, it will be fine.

90
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:45,842
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you not... How do you... How are you not mentally affected?

91
00:06:45,862 --> 00:06:46,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can reflect.

92
00:06:47,063 --> 00:06:48,704
[SPEAKER_00]: How would it be to endure elements?

93
00:06:49,324 --> 00:06:51,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Endure the heat or cold?

94
00:06:51,066 --> 00:06:53,067
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know, well, expose yourself to it a bit.

95
00:06:54,096 --> 00:06:58,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't pursue comfort, physical comfort, all the time in your life.

96
00:06:58,778 --> 00:07:05,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Experience some discomfort, and then see how mind rebels against it, how it wants to make you out, how it wants to get rid of it.

97
00:07:05,502 --> 00:07:09,564
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same attitude towards harsh speech, or towards random insults.

98
00:07:09,704 --> 00:07:13,906
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't really go and try and find harsh... Well, you don't have to really go and find it.

99
00:07:14,826 --> 00:07:15,847
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you just need to...

100
00:07:16,347 --> 00:07:17,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to come to you sooner or later.

101
00:07:18,508 --> 00:07:22,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in that sense, yeah, what you can go and find is physical discomfort.

102
00:07:22,750 --> 00:07:25,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That the mind doesn't want to endure with, doesn't want to deal with.

103
00:07:27,014 --> 00:07:30,597
[SPEAKER_00]: And by training in regard to that, you're training in regard to the mental discomfort as well.

104
00:07:31,838 --> 00:07:37,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when the harsh speech does come, well, you're already trained from not reacting.

105
00:07:38,072 --> 00:07:52,559
[SPEAKER_01]: But some people can endure, you know, the elements, extreme elements, push themselves to limits, but when it comes... when someone challenges their views, or their nationality, or whatever they believe, or God doesn't exist, then there's a fight.

106
00:07:52,639 --> 00:07:57,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why it's not like... you can't just go and endure elements and expect wisdom to come out of it.

107
00:07:57,562 --> 00:07:59,142
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to know what you're supposed to look for.

108
00:08:01,223 --> 00:08:01,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm saying.

109
00:08:02,444 --> 00:08:06,466
[SPEAKER_00]: If you know what you're supposed... if you know that the nature of being insulted, being challenged,

110
00:08:07,019 --> 00:08:14,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, on no particular ground, just gratuitous insult or offense, that equals enduring the elements.

111
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:20,942
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't know that, you won't look for that when you endure the elements, which means then you won't be trained in regard to it.

112
00:08:21,422 --> 00:08:24,103
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something to do with taking things personally.

113
00:08:24,583 --> 00:08:25,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, absolutely.

114
00:08:25,203 --> 00:08:25,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

115
00:08:25,603 --> 00:08:26,643
[SPEAKER_00]: With the weather, with the elements.

116
00:08:26,663 --> 00:08:29,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, feeling, feeling like, like, feeling entitled to

117
00:08:31,162 --> 00:08:39,331
[SPEAKER_00]: to break away from discomfort, for example, in physical terms, or taking things personally when somebody verbally says something to you that you don't like.

118
00:08:39,851 --> 00:08:42,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't help but take it personally.

119
00:08:42,194 --> 00:08:44,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can help, but you haven't been training yourself to help.

120
00:08:45,538 --> 00:08:49,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So now the habit is just take it personally and then just lash out.

121
00:08:49,926 --> 00:08:51,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, he deserves it.

122
00:08:51,628 --> 00:08:55,291
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I mean, people can take that so far when, you know, when you get the the outraged culture.

123
00:08:55,792 --> 00:08:56,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in Europe, for example.

124
00:08:57,213 --> 00:08:58,794
[SPEAKER_00]: They've witnessed, obviously.

125
00:09:00,336 --> 00:09:04,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just people get outraged, furious, over things that doesn't... don't even concern them.

126
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:08,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that doesn't mean, oh, I shouldn't do anything about rectifying wrong.

127
00:09:08,864 --> 00:09:11,727
[SPEAKER_00]: But you shouldn't get angry on account of random wrong.

128
00:09:12,387 --> 00:09:12,748
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not.

129
00:09:12,848 --> 00:09:15,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Rectifying the wrong can be done without the anger.

130
00:09:16,234 --> 00:09:20,977
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're acting out of anger, then the purpose of your action is just acting out of anger.

131
00:09:21,017 --> 00:09:25,160
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of lofty goal can uproot that anger and make it wholesome.

132
00:09:29,302 --> 00:09:30,563
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, is there enough light?

133
00:09:30,843 --> 00:09:33,085
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not too dark on the screen.

134
00:09:34,666 --> 00:09:35,766
[SPEAKER_01]: It's getting a bit darker.

135
00:09:36,067 --> 00:09:36,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

136
00:09:47,932 --> 00:09:59,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the correct response, then, to an aggressive person?

137
00:09:59,974 --> 00:10:03,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically aggressive or verbally aggressive?

138
00:10:04,835 --> 00:10:05,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, both.

139
00:10:07,976 --> 00:10:15,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, if your life is physically threatened, again, you don't have to... Obviously, you know, we're not talking about killing or harming.

140
00:10:16,427 --> 00:10:21,490
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can subdue the person to give yourself enough room to escape, that's fine.

141
00:10:22,891 --> 00:10:24,472
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can just escape, that's even better.

142
00:10:26,394 --> 00:10:29,336
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it comes to verbally aggressive, again, same.

143
00:10:29,776 --> 00:10:33,438
[SPEAKER_00]: The first... the main response is to not respond.

144
00:10:34,579 --> 00:10:41,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And recognize, well, there is no communication here, obviously, so you don't try to change your mind, so you just remove yourself from that situation.

145
00:10:44,530 --> 00:10:47,911
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the simile of the saw that the Buddha gave, you know?

146
00:10:48,092 --> 00:10:49,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's when you can't escape.

147
00:10:49,672 --> 00:10:49,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

148
00:10:50,653 --> 00:10:53,614
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you cannot escape and they catch you and start sawing your limbs.

149
00:10:53,634 --> 00:10:54,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, even then, you cannot escape.

150
00:10:54,935 --> 00:10:59,557
[SPEAKER_00]: The simile of the saw when highway robbers saw you limb by limb, savagely.

151
00:11:00,997 --> 00:11:11,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it doesn't mean you don't feel any pain, but the arahant, the enlightened person, the one who has fulfilled the Buddha's teaching, he doesn't feel anger towards them on account of such extreme action.

152
00:11:12,822 --> 00:11:14,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is something you should train for.

153
00:11:15,405 --> 00:11:16,305
[SPEAKER_00]: People will dismiss that.

154
00:11:16,405 --> 00:11:17,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's a tall order.

155
00:11:17,625 --> 00:11:23,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you don't aim for a tall order, how can you expect to arrive at anything, if you've made a compromise before you even started practicing?

156
00:11:24,727 --> 00:11:26,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just contemplate.

157
00:11:26,407 --> 00:11:29,167
[SPEAKER_00]: How would it be, how would you feel if somebody physically attacks you?

158
00:11:30,247 --> 00:11:31,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, life-threatening attack.

159
00:11:32,208 --> 00:11:33,048
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't get away.

160
00:11:33,248 --> 00:11:35,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't get away, and you can't defend yourself.

161
00:11:35,348 --> 00:11:38,969
[SPEAKER_00]: They're more, greater in number, stronger than you.

162
00:11:40,149 --> 00:11:40,729
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you feel?

163
00:11:40,769 --> 00:11:42,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Would your mind be overwhelmed by that experience?

164
00:11:43,410 --> 00:11:44,930
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, there is a work for you.

165
00:11:45,544 --> 00:11:47,445
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to go and seek out the danger.

166
00:11:47,765 --> 00:11:53,029
[SPEAKER_00]: You just think about it and it will bring the experience if it's sufficient enough to be able to contemplate.

167
00:11:53,069 --> 00:12:03,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It does say about pervading the world with kindness, you know.

168
00:12:03,195 --> 00:12:06,337
[SPEAKER_01]: One should train in pervading the world with kindness.

169
00:12:06,817 --> 00:12:08,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Pervading the world.

170
00:12:08,098 --> 00:12:08,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

171
00:12:08,458 --> 00:12:15,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Pervading your experience of the world with non-ill-will, which is what kindness is.

172
00:12:16,293 --> 00:12:18,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like aiming at a person individually.

173
00:12:18,715 --> 00:12:20,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I love you, I love you, I love you too.

174
00:12:21,617 --> 00:12:30,824
[SPEAKER_00]: No, pervading with kindness means being incapable of ill-will, towards the framework on the basis of which experience of others manifests.

175
00:12:31,965 --> 00:12:37,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind has discerned the phenomenon of ill-will and anger and has stopped entertaining it,

176
00:12:38,358 --> 00:12:44,943
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of people or circumstances coming your way with anger will be able to provoke you angrily.

177
00:12:45,704 --> 00:12:47,105
[SPEAKER_00]: That means your mind is above anger.

178
00:12:47,425 --> 00:12:48,286
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the definition.

179
00:12:48,546 --> 00:12:50,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do then, it will be kind.

180
00:12:50,648 --> 00:12:53,089
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, it will be not based in your will.

181
00:12:56,992 --> 00:13:05,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say, someone has already harmed you.

182
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:06,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

183
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:11,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been harmed in the past and... Well, who... what has been harmed in the past?

184
00:13:11,971 --> 00:13:13,391
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

185
00:13:13,411 --> 00:13:13,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, me.

186
00:13:14,231 --> 00:13:15,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what part of me?

187
00:13:17,932 --> 00:13:20,712
[SPEAKER_00]: What can other person relate to, when they relate with you?

188
00:13:21,252 --> 00:13:21,993
[SPEAKER_00]: What can they see?

189
00:13:22,253 --> 00:13:23,253
[SPEAKER_00]: What can they aim at?

190
00:13:23,353 --> 00:13:24,773
[SPEAKER_00]: What can they harm or not harm?

191
00:13:24,893 --> 00:13:25,293
[SPEAKER_01]: My body.

192
00:13:25,473 --> 00:13:25,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

193
00:13:26,913 --> 00:13:29,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the body, that you appeared with, has been harmed.

194
00:13:30,514 --> 00:13:32,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't help but take it personally.

195
00:13:32,614 --> 00:13:33,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thus, I am...

196
00:13:34,215 --> 00:13:34,995
[SPEAKER_00]: I was harmed.

197
00:13:36,015 --> 00:13:36,615
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's on you.

198
00:13:37,543 --> 00:13:47,565
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to... not being... body being harmed is not on you, but you taking that body, which can be harmed, or was harmed, or could be harmed, that's on you.

199
00:13:47,805 --> 00:13:53,326
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take that which doesn't belong to you, and because of that, you make yourself liable to harm.

200
00:13:55,066 --> 00:14:03,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it comes from this self-entitlement that we all have, obviously, especially when you, you know, you just live a normal life, without any effort to develop your mind.

201
00:14:04,127 --> 00:14:06,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you think you have every right to pleasure, to sense pleasures,

202
00:14:07,291 --> 00:14:11,293
[SPEAKER_00]: You have every right to pursuit of satisfaction and you have every right to avoid pain.

203
00:14:12,874 --> 00:14:14,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That's only true in a relative sense.

204
00:14:15,135 --> 00:14:20,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, you don't have right to that which can experience pleasure or experience pain.

205
00:14:21,198 --> 00:14:23,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Namely, your senses.

206
00:14:23,179 --> 00:14:23,880
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not yours.

207
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:25,981
[SPEAKER_00]: You're paid with, they're given, you borrow them.

208
00:14:26,961 --> 00:14:28,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you act like you're their master.

209
00:14:29,483 --> 00:14:31,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when they don't go your way, yeah...

210
00:14:32,191 --> 00:14:37,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mastery, the core of your very being is very compromised, and that's very unpleasant.

211
00:14:38,813 --> 00:14:44,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you forgive the past insults or harm done to you.

212
00:14:44,614 --> 00:14:50,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that it cannot be done to you, as in to your point of view, to your experience as a whole.

213
00:14:50,355 --> 00:14:56,997
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only be done to one aspect of it, that others can perceive and relate to, that you don't need to take personally.

214
00:14:58,189 --> 00:15:03,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying that is the... that is the... and what it sounds... obviously, what it sounds like, that is the only cure.

215
00:15:05,431 --> 00:15:05,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Truly, yeah.

216
00:15:05,952 --> 00:15:06,832
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a true forgiveness.

217
00:15:07,432 --> 00:15:07,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

218
00:15:08,092 --> 00:15:10,073
[SPEAKER_01]: For overcoming past... Yeah, yeah.

219
00:15:10,093 --> 00:15:15,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, overcoming past, overcoming future liability, overcoming present, there is a thing.

220
00:15:15,075 --> 00:15:15,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

221
00:15:15,395 --> 00:15:16,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Safety for the people.

222
00:15:16,115 --> 00:15:16,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

223
00:15:17,075 --> 00:15:17,575
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

224
00:15:17,635 --> 00:15:20,997
[SPEAKER_00]: If the dhamma is applied correctly, you don't have to...

225
00:15:21,057 --> 00:15:25,398
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, obviously, you have to do it repetitively, as in training, training yourself and develop it.

226
00:15:25,879 --> 00:15:31,701
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's developed rightly, it affects present and past through present and future through that present.

227
00:15:32,992 --> 00:15:47,402
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, one who understands that not-self, not-myself, he understands that, he has that insight, he also understands the past, so he stops thinking, oh, I was in the past, now I'm not anymore, or I will not be in the future, I will be in the future.

228
00:15:47,422 --> 00:15:53,325
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that the whole domain of temporal existence is uprooted by this understanding.

229
00:15:54,626 --> 00:16:01,591
[SPEAKER_00]: That whether it was the past, future, present, it's not mine, it could have not been mine.

230
00:16:06,050 --> 00:16:07,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, revenge is not an option?

231
00:16:08,471 --> 00:16:09,212
[SPEAKER_00]: No, absolutely.

232
00:16:09,252 --> 00:16:31,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Because revenge, engaging in revenge, means engaging upon the act that's fully founded in the perversion of that principle of not mine, which means you took it as mine, which means it implies revenge, retaliation, saying back with ill-will, it implies that taking up as mine, taking up as I can act upon this.

233
00:16:32,405 --> 00:16:37,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, how can you undo it, if you keep acting wrongly out of it, and you maintain that wrong order?

234
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:53,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the most common questions for a human being is, why am I here?

235
00:16:54,549 --> 00:16:58,093
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, how did this universe begin?

236
00:16:58,293 --> 00:17:00,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Who created the world?

237
00:17:00,656 --> 00:17:02,498
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my meaning of life?

238
00:17:03,459 --> 00:17:04,020
[SPEAKER_01]: What's going on?

239
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:04,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Where am I going?

240
00:17:04,941 --> 00:17:05,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do I come from?

241
00:17:06,643 --> 00:17:12,829
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you read the suttas, those questions are wrong, in a sense.

242
00:17:14,751 --> 00:17:15,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are they wrong?

243
00:17:15,431 --> 00:17:17,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are they wrong?

244
00:17:18,174 --> 00:17:29,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they come out of that mistake of assuming the ownership, mastery, assuming the master self of the experience.

245
00:17:29,164 --> 00:17:31,807
[SPEAKER_00]: They come out of that wrong order that I was just talking about.

246
00:17:32,683 --> 00:17:34,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where did I come from?

247
00:17:35,045 --> 00:17:36,346
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my purpose?

248
00:17:36,487 --> 00:17:40,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all based on that self-center, or the center of self.

249
00:17:40,871 --> 00:17:44,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And as such, providing any answer just affirms the wrong order.

250
00:17:45,095 --> 00:17:53,963
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if somebody comes with the wrong premise and asks you a question, whereby if you answer it, well, you give the validity to the premise that they wrongly assumed as a basis of the question.

251
00:17:55,346 --> 00:17:56,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha just wouldn't answer.

252
00:17:56,967 --> 00:17:59,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he would say, he would give the paticca-sampada.

253
00:17:59,728 --> 00:18:01,749
[SPEAKER_00]: He would say, with the rising of this, this arises.

254
00:18:02,149 --> 00:18:06,330
[SPEAKER_00]: With the rising of ignorance, determinations manifest, and so on.

255
00:18:06,731 --> 00:18:07,771
[SPEAKER_00]: And how does that relate to?

256
00:18:07,931 --> 00:18:08,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

257
00:18:08,491 --> 00:18:10,492
[SPEAKER_00]: It relates to directly explaining you.

258
00:18:10,632 --> 00:18:20,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not explaining, directly describing you that principle of perversion of paticca-sampada with the grain or paticca-sampada against the grain, if it's developed properly.

259
00:18:21,333 --> 00:18:27,336
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would undo that wrong order, simultaneously present wrong order of you taking that which is not yours as mine.

260
00:18:28,617 --> 00:18:29,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And then those questions would cease.

261
00:18:29,998 --> 00:18:30,798
[SPEAKER_00]: They would be meaningless.

262
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:37,662
[SPEAKER_01]: What questions would you have instead, if those questions became... Why do I suffer?

263
00:18:38,743 --> 00:18:41,644
[SPEAKER_00]: How can I free myself from suffering?

264
00:18:42,285 --> 00:18:47,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I really a master of my experience, in control of my life?

265
00:18:47,087 --> 00:18:48,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I not get sick?

266
00:18:48,808 --> 00:18:49,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I not be aging?

267
00:18:50,966 --> 00:18:52,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I make sure my heart never stops?

268
00:18:53,467 --> 00:18:54,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I not get cancer?

269
00:18:55,147 --> 00:18:55,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I not die?

270
00:19:00,009 --> 00:19:01,869
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the importance.

271
00:19:02,209 --> 00:19:10,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, fundamentally, just keep asking yourself, in regard to whatever you do, just keep asking yourself, why do I want to do this?

272
00:19:10,072 --> 00:19:11,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I asking this question?

273
00:19:11,792 --> 00:19:12,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that.

274
00:19:12,273 --> 00:19:13,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I asking myself this?

275
00:19:15,800 --> 00:19:21,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the world, the senses pull you out, they direct you out, they entice you out with pleasures and so on.

276
00:19:22,850 --> 00:19:30,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Asking these questions would keep sort of pointing back inwards, but not in some sort of mystifying sense, but in a sense of looking for answers.

277
00:19:43,318 --> 00:19:50,800
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with questions like what happens after death, the body dies... Again, any notion of death that you can have is based on this life.

278
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:53,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's not really death, that which death is.

279
00:19:54,782 --> 00:19:58,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you're asking a question based on what you think death is, what happens after it.

280
00:19:58,883 --> 00:20:00,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's doubly meaningless.

281
00:20:05,705 --> 00:20:11,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, trying to understand how the workings of the karma and vipāka, energies and whatnot,

282
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:15,421
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, that's just gonna result in distraction or madness.

283
00:20:16,121 --> 00:20:18,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't fathom it, because you don't need to.

284
00:20:20,302 --> 00:20:21,702
[SPEAKER_00]: How the saṃsāra operates.

285
00:20:25,663 --> 00:20:29,724
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead, ask yourself, why do I want to know that?

286
00:20:29,764 --> 00:20:32,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I seek this form of knowledge?

287
00:20:32,884 --> 00:20:34,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I need that information?

288
00:20:34,545 --> 00:20:35,025
[SPEAKER_00]: What for?

289
00:20:35,585 --> 00:20:37,385
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the motivation behind?

290
00:20:37,785 --> 00:20:41,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll see the motivation behind every single action you ever done, you ever...

291
00:20:41,784 --> 00:20:46,566
[SPEAKER_00]: What you will ever do and you're doing is motivated in how you feel or how you want to feel.

292
00:20:47,526 --> 00:20:47,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

293
00:20:49,267 --> 00:20:51,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, ah, okay, so what if I overcome the feelings?

294
00:20:52,588 --> 00:20:54,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there's nothing for me to do anymore.

295
00:20:54,669 --> 00:20:56,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, if someone's looking for...

296
00:20:57,090 --> 00:21:00,392
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to find out if there is a God, of God created.

297
00:21:00,872 --> 00:21:01,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

298
00:21:01,232 --> 00:21:02,572
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my mission.

299
00:21:03,013 --> 00:21:03,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

300
00:21:03,753 --> 00:21:05,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying, then...

301
00:21:05,494 --> 00:21:08,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself, why is that... why did you make that a mission of yours?

302
00:21:10,010 --> 00:21:12,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I find out that there is a God... What's gonna happen?

303
00:21:13,131 --> 00:21:13,731
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll feel great.

304
00:21:14,071 --> 00:21:14,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

305
00:21:15,551 --> 00:21:21,652
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll feel satisfied, I'll feel at ease, I'll feel... You will feel pleasant.

306
00:21:22,672 --> 00:21:32,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you realize that if you want to do something, your actions should be aimed at understanding the motivation for you wanting to do things in the first place.

307
00:21:32,854 --> 00:21:36,535
[SPEAKER_00]: As in understanding motivation for your feeling being the reason for your action.

308
00:21:37,135 --> 00:21:38,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings being the reason for your action.

309
00:21:41,356 --> 00:21:47,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, in terms of... if people want to know what is the... how did the world begin?

310
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:48,721
[SPEAKER_01]: How did the universe begin?

311
00:21:48,961 --> 00:21:57,966
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason they want to know that is because they think that that answer will provide a satisfaction which is a form of pleasure, almost... but on a level of a view, almost.

312
00:21:58,667 --> 00:22:03,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, they have a view that they can apply throughout their life, as everything will be fine.

313
00:22:04,110 --> 00:22:05,131
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people want to have

314
00:22:06,103 --> 00:22:17,869
[SPEAKER_00]: The God exists in the heaven and stuff, just to provide that utterly, utmost general pleasure that they can use to sort of go through life without fear and anxiety.

315
00:22:18,890 --> 00:22:24,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But, what they fail to see, because it's very unpleasant to see it, is that that pleasure is still based on this life.

316
00:22:25,393 --> 00:22:30,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you don't really... you cannot really know anything different from it.

317
00:22:30,636 --> 00:22:34,978
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot really know, oh yes, there is heaven after, or there is God, or there isn't a God.

318
00:22:36,818 --> 00:22:39,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're fully enclosed within yourself and within your own experience.

319
00:22:40,599 --> 00:22:48,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that... that honesty that's necessary to recognize that actually certain things cannot be known, eh... based on this experience.

320
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:51,801
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot know how it would be to be outside of this experience.

321
00:22:52,181 --> 00:22:55,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And even when you think about outside of this experience, that's within your experience.

322
00:22:56,482 --> 00:23:01,623
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, all the views that you hold on account of it, to, like, reinterpret the life back to yourself.

323
00:23:02,690 --> 00:23:04,512
[SPEAKER_00]: which is a form of regaining the control.

324
00:23:05,032 --> 00:23:10,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, this is how the universe started, this is how the universe will end, this is heaven, this is hell.

325
00:23:10,578 --> 00:23:15,503
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a form of greater control over that which you know deep down you're not not in control of.

326
00:23:16,304 --> 00:23:17,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're liable to feelings.

327
00:23:18,987 --> 00:23:21,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings of fear and unpleasantness and so on.

328
00:23:29,817 --> 00:23:34,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Like science, you know, looking into atom...

329
00:23:35,279 --> 00:23:35,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

330
00:23:35,980 --> 00:23:38,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Science looking into small atom...

331
00:23:38,841 --> 00:23:39,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

332
00:23:39,021 --> 00:23:40,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking into distant horizons...

333
00:23:40,742 --> 00:23:41,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

334
00:23:41,122 --> 00:23:44,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Measuring the universe and the stars, yeah.

335
00:23:45,343 --> 00:23:46,444
[SPEAKER_01]: That can bring benefit.

336
00:23:47,104 --> 00:23:56,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it can bring benefit in terms of information about the world, about the matter of your experience, but not information about the

337
00:23:58,439 --> 00:24:10,934
[SPEAKER_00]: The tools you use to get that information, which is the tools of your senses, your mind, your thought, your intentionality, your feelings, your views, you can't reinterpret them through things that you discover based on them.

338
00:24:11,154 --> 00:24:12,156
[SPEAKER_00]: They're secondary to it.

339
00:24:13,057 --> 00:24:16,561
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said you won't reach the universe by traveling.

340
00:24:18,019 --> 00:24:23,203
[SPEAKER_00]: To the end of the world or something, the beginning and the end of universe is in this very body, nowhere else.

341
00:24:23,503 --> 00:24:23,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

342
00:24:23,923 --> 00:24:27,066
[SPEAKER_00]: This very experience as a whole, that's your entire universe.

343
00:24:27,566 --> 00:24:33,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And no matter how far you go in the universe, imagine, could you go without this body being there, present?

344
00:24:34,672 --> 00:24:35,072
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't.

345
00:24:35,553 --> 00:24:39,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you even imagine it, abstractly, without this body being still alive here?

346
00:24:39,876 --> 00:24:40,277
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't.

347
00:24:40,377 --> 00:24:41,578
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're fully enclosed in it.

348
00:24:42,270 --> 00:24:45,212
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point that's useful to contemplate.

349
00:24:45,712 --> 00:24:49,935
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you're bound with it as in you're tied to a post and then you can move around the post.

350
00:24:50,456 --> 00:24:50,596
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

351
00:24:50,696 --> 00:24:51,456
[SPEAKER_00]: You're within it.

352
00:24:51,957 --> 00:24:53,338
[SPEAKER_00]: The body's the container.

353
00:24:53,418 --> 00:24:54,839
[SPEAKER_00]: The senses are your container.

354
00:24:55,719 --> 00:24:59,022
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates are the container that you cannot conceive the outside of.

355
00:24:59,982 --> 00:25:00,723
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

356
00:25:00,743 --> 00:25:03,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people in meditation who would develop memory,

357
00:25:04,348 --> 00:25:11,753
[SPEAKER_00]: of the previous life, one previous life, five previous lives, five hundred previous lives, all they would remember is these five aggregates or one among them.

358
00:25:12,894 --> 00:25:13,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't step outside of it.

359
00:25:14,135 --> 00:25:15,496
[SPEAKER_00]: And Arjuna doesn't step outside of it.

360
00:25:15,896 --> 00:25:20,039
[SPEAKER_00]: He removes the slightest trace of ownership in regard to that experience as a whole.

361
00:25:21,700 --> 00:25:28,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Slightest trace of ownership in regard to feelings, in regard to matter, feeling, perception, intentions, and, and, and consciousness.

362
00:25:30,286 --> 00:25:32,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how he disappears, so to speak.

363
00:25:34,811 --> 00:25:51,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any direct method to overcome this sense of ownership?

364
00:25:51,518 --> 00:25:55,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate, through your ownership, that which you own.

365
00:25:57,140 --> 00:26:03,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, take up that which you regard as yours, and then see how

366
00:26:05,683 --> 00:26:20,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, the body, take up the body, my body, is that sense of my body, is that the center of it?

367
00:26:20,910 --> 00:26:32,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, would this body be depending upon all these individual organs within that sense of ownership of this body, that are required to be operating, that I cannot control?

368
00:26:36,517 --> 00:26:43,923
[SPEAKER_00]: If those organs were to fail, would I be able to entertain the notion of this body is mine if this body dies right... right in front of me?

369
00:26:43,943 --> 00:26:46,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's... You realize that you couldn't.

370
00:26:46,905 --> 00:26:55,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're perverting the order by regarding... by giving the priority of that sense of self, of my master, of this body.

371
00:26:55,972 --> 00:27:00,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you think about the individual organs, you don't think about them within that sense of master.

372
00:27:00,495 --> 00:27:02,457
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about left, right, before or after.

373
00:27:02,817 --> 00:27:03,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you don't uproot it.

374
00:27:05,353 --> 00:27:10,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the closest to a method would be to contemplate within this ownership.

375
00:27:11,740 --> 00:27:17,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't leave the ownership out and contemplate things rightly, because the whole point of contemplation is to experience the non-ownership.

376
00:27:20,014 --> 00:27:36,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And you experience the non-ownership when you see that this body, no matter how much it's in your control, its heart is rooted in that which you cannot even conceive controlling, such as heart, breathing lungs, pumping blood, operating organs, functioning brain.

377
00:27:36,805 --> 00:27:41,728
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even conceive how it would be to control those things or have say in their operating.

378
00:27:41,808 --> 00:27:49,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, they are the fundamental basis for the notion of your body as a whole that you do own.

379
00:27:50,208 --> 00:27:50,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Mistaken.

380
00:27:50,868 --> 00:27:54,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Because we think we come, we, myself, comes first.

381
00:27:55,511 --> 00:27:56,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah.

382
00:27:56,612 --> 00:27:58,973
[SPEAKER_00]: I come first, thus this is for me, this is mine.

383
00:27:59,393 --> 00:28:00,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

384
00:28:00,174 --> 00:28:03,755
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because I take all this to be mine, I develop a sense of I am.

385
00:28:06,077 --> 00:28:13,541
[SPEAKER_00]: But that sense of I am can be undone, not by denying it, or by saying, oh, there's no self, there's not really me, look, everything is changing, this is not... No.

386
00:28:13,761 --> 00:28:15,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to take up that sense of I am,

387
00:28:16,251 --> 00:28:20,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, see that it's founded upon things that cannot be yours.

388
00:28:20,693 --> 00:28:24,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, automatically, your experience is the experience of not-I-am.

389
00:28:24,635 --> 00:28:25,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Reversing the mistakes.

390
00:28:26,516 --> 00:28:28,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, draining, basically, draining them.

391
00:28:28,557 --> 00:28:33,720
[SPEAKER_00]: The kind of the structure stays the same, but what has drained away is that appropriation from it.

392
00:28:35,500 --> 00:28:38,102
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, for an arhant, it's like a palm stump.

393
00:28:39,046 --> 00:28:39,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Cut at its root.

394
00:28:40,267 --> 00:28:43,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the stump, the branches that grown, they stayed, but now they're then done.

395
00:28:43,690 --> 00:28:44,350
[SPEAKER_00]: They're petrified.

396
00:28:44,670 --> 00:28:50,275
[SPEAKER_00]: They're still there, as the way person's experience might have proliferated throughout the ignorant life.

397
00:28:51,275 --> 00:29:01,003
[SPEAKER_00]: But, once you uproot that ignorance, once you remove that appropriation, it stops proliferating and then just stays like a palm stump, until even that breaks apart, in part, in a bundle.

398
00:29:02,117 --> 00:29:12,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you lose that or get rid of that sense of ownership, your personality doesn't... how does your personality

399
00:29:13,518 --> 00:29:18,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, anything in your personality that was depending upon the ownership, that will disappear.

400
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:21,261
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are other things that were not depending upon ownership.

401
00:29:21,281 --> 00:29:22,961
[SPEAKER_00]: They were depending upon, like, hollow habits.

402
00:29:23,681 --> 00:29:25,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Habits that were not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

403
00:29:26,022 --> 00:29:26,522
[SPEAKER_00]: That stays.

404
00:29:27,543 --> 00:29:32,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as I said many times, and in the suttas you read often, people cannot tell an arahant externally.

405
00:29:34,545 --> 00:29:38,406
[SPEAKER_00]: And even another arahant cannot come and see the, I don't know, some special aura around him.

406
00:29:38,486 --> 00:29:39,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he's an arahant.

407
00:29:39,126 --> 00:29:39,246
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

408
00:29:39,567 --> 00:29:40,807
[SPEAKER_00]: He can tell an arahant when they start

409
00:29:41,109 --> 00:29:45,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Discussing each other's understanding of dhamma, and then you can see, oh, yeah, he understood this, probably to the extent.

410
00:29:46,173 --> 00:29:55,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, then the Buddha, or somebody with very, very developed mind, could see little traits, little things, based on which he could tell the other person's mind.

411
00:29:56,401 --> 00:29:57,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But on an average, you can't.

412
00:29:57,602 --> 00:30:01,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Even like Sariputta couldn't tell, in some cases, that the person was an arahant.

413
00:30:02,126 --> 00:30:03,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Then he would go to the Buddha and said,

414
00:30:03,640 --> 00:30:04,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Where was his rebirth?

415
00:30:04,561 --> 00:30:05,241
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, what do you mean?

416
00:30:05,421 --> 00:30:06,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't he say he was an arahant?

417
00:30:06,582 --> 00:30:11,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he did, but he was associating with some... not some reputable families and so on.

418
00:30:11,266 --> 00:30:13,368
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, that's... that's not why a person is blameworthy.

419
00:30:13,388 --> 00:30:21,073
[SPEAKER_00]: A person is blameworthy because they leave this body, they take another body, as in they keep... remain bound with saṃsāra.

420
00:30:21,133 --> 00:30:22,234
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can tell you he wasn't.

421
00:30:22,875 --> 00:30:23,695
[SPEAKER_00]: He wasn't blameworthy.

422
00:30:23,715 --> 00:30:25,377
[SPEAKER_00]: He was an arahant even before he died.

423
00:30:26,197 --> 00:30:27,118
[SPEAKER_00]: But everybody couldn't see it.

424
00:30:29,085 --> 00:30:38,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the... or the other, that famous one, who was like really harsh in his speech, and the people were often offended, and then the Buddha said, oh, it's his old habits.

425
00:30:38,510 --> 00:30:44,694
[SPEAKER_00]: They were not rooted in ill will or greed or anything like that, but obviously, he would come across as very angry, furious.

426
00:30:45,314 --> 00:30:54,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, that doesn't mean like, oh, okay, so I don't have to... You will know that... you will know which habits can stay once you're an arahant or on the way of becoming one.

427
00:30:54,540 --> 00:30:56,001
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't decide that beforehand.

428
00:30:56,081 --> 00:30:56,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, these are my

429
00:30:57,442 --> 00:30:58,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Neutral habits.

430
00:30:58,823 --> 00:31:01,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to take them all with caution.

431
00:31:01,105 --> 00:31:03,927
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're in a state of kutujana, you can't trust any of your habits.

432
00:31:04,527 --> 00:31:07,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to keep investigating any of it, if it's rooted on greed, aversion, delusion.

433
00:31:08,811 --> 00:31:14,335
[SPEAKER_00]: And truly, truly, when you dismantle everything that shouldn't be there, whatever's left, then you know that can be left.

434
00:31:15,710 --> 00:31:21,434
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very different from saying that the self doesn't exist.

435
00:31:22,174 --> 00:31:23,275
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no self.

436
00:31:23,395 --> 00:31:24,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the Buddha never said that.

437
00:31:24,936 --> 00:31:26,457
[SPEAKER_00]: When he asked him, does the self exist?

438
00:31:26,497 --> 00:31:27,077
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't say it.

439
00:31:27,097 --> 00:31:28,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the self not exist?

440
00:31:28,278 --> 00:31:29,079
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't answer it.

441
00:31:30,159 --> 00:31:31,300
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's a shortcut.

442
00:31:31,520 --> 00:31:35,703
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a quick fix in terms of satisfying explanation.

443
00:31:35,803 --> 00:31:37,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a contemplation when you say there's no self.

444
00:31:38,405 --> 00:31:41,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, all these rocks, they're constantly changing electrons and atoms.

445
00:31:41,407 --> 00:31:42,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, nothing is real.

446
00:31:42,247 --> 00:31:43,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, there is no self.

447
00:31:43,088 --> 00:31:44,409
[SPEAKER_00]: This is just a hollow convention.

448
00:31:45,599 --> 00:31:47,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Conventional speech and so on.

449
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:49,021
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's that's not what Buddha said.

450
00:31:49,881 --> 00:31:50,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can see that.

451
00:31:50,662 --> 00:31:52,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Because conventions are real as such.

452
00:31:53,063 --> 00:31:57,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you might not call it self, but the thing is that phenomena that you refer to yourself, it's there.

453
00:31:57,706 --> 00:32:00,767
[SPEAKER_00]: If it weren't there, you wouldn't be able to refer to it whatsoever.

454
00:32:02,668 --> 00:32:06,431
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dismissing the reality does not remove your ownership of that reality.

455
00:32:09,292 --> 00:32:12,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, ah, so I should be concerned with me appropriating reality

456
00:32:13,068 --> 00:32:17,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being concerned about dismantling conventions and proving that nothing exists.

457
00:32:20,413 --> 00:32:26,738
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, instead of treating the illness in regard to the body, you just go and chop up the body.

458
00:32:26,758 --> 00:32:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And say, see, the body can be chopped up.

459
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:29,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's not real.

460
00:32:30,581 --> 00:32:31,802
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it is real.

461
00:32:32,022 --> 00:32:34,023
[SPEAKER_00]: As a collection of parts that can be chopped up.

462
00:32:34,644 --> 00:32:35,304
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a reality.

463
00:32:36,125 --> 00:32:39,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was never a problem.

464
00:32:39,848 --> 00:32:41,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is that self.

465
00:32:41,449 --> 00:32:42,610
[SPEAKER_00]: There is that sense of self.

466
00:32:44,031 --> 00:32:53,895
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that there is no self, but what you should do is remove the ownership of that sense of self, and then it becomes anatta, as in not-myself.

467
00:33:00,198 --> 00:33:01,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a practice of denial.

468
00:33:02,859 --> 00:33:06,561
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, I don't want to say not-myself, because then people might assume, ah, someone else's self.

469
00:33:07,261 --> 00:33:07,401
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

470
00:33:07,642 --> 00:33:08,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Anatta is

471
00:33:09,359 --> 00:33:12,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Self that you don't own, and that cannot be called self.

472
00:33:13,322 --> 00:33:14,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not self.

473
00:33:15,663 --> 00:33:19,406
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that which was taken as self, still there, but now it's not.

474
00:33:19,646 --> 00:33:20,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Taken is not self.

475
00:33:22,388 --> 00:33:28,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But people are concerned with denying that which is taken as self, which is an aspect of their experience, one way or the other.

476
00:33:29,592 --> 00:33:36,137
[SPEAKER_00]: But as the Buddha said, the five aggregates are not the assumption, are not the holding the view of the self.

477
00:33:36,917 --> 00:33:39,658
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no view of the Self apart from the Five Aggregates.

478
00:33:40,558 --> 00:33:44,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's desire and lust in regard to the Five Aggregates that the view of Self is there.

479
00:33:44,660 --> 00:33:53,003
[SPEAKER_00]: By you coming and saying there is no Self, the Five Aggregates are impermanent, there is no Self, you're just dismissing it, the problem, and without actually understanding it.

480
00:33:53,043 --> 00:34:00,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were to... if you were to truly understand it, well, the only way to truly understand it is to free yourself from that Self, to see it as not-Self.

481
00:34:00,866 --> 00:34:04,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any explanation that you think you understood, unless it's freeing you from that sense of Self,

482
00:34:08,182 --> 00:34:13,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people can put a lot of time and effort in denial practice.

483
00:34:13,424 --> 00:34:14,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, unfortunately.

484
00:34:14,945 --> 00:34:18,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Many of the meditation methods and techniques are all based on that view.

485
00:34:18,546 --> 00:34:25,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Just denial and seeing the moment-to-moment movement, they will kind of show you that somehow things are not really real, cannot be yours.

486
00:34:25,829 --> 00:34:29,751
[SPEAKER_01]: But that whole practice goes completely against being

487
00:34:31,565 --> 00:34:37,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Self-transparent, honest, authentic, saying this, I do experience this self, I do experience this thing.

488
00:34:37,709 --> 00:34:40,310
[SPEAKER_00]: There is mother, there is father, there is given, there is offered.

489
00:34:40,350 --> 00:34:41,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Or there's no gender, there's no gender.

490
00:34:42,171 --> 00:34:44,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, okay, people can deny anything, whatever they please.

491
00:34:46,148 --> 00:34:48,110
[SPEAKER_00]: As the attitude, yeah, it can be aimed literally at anything.

492
00:34:48,130 --> 00:34:56,216
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said the right... the worldly right view is the view that acknowledges the validity of random phenomena, of phenomena in your experience.

493
00:34:56,256 --> 00:35:14,451
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there is this, there is that, there is given, there is the... there is mother, there is father, there is the liberation, there is the power, allowing... Like, you don't need to see them, and of course, you don't see them at that level, but you need to know that there is a possibility of them being... being real, as in there is a possibility of being free.

494
00:35:15,990 --> 00:35:20,213
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you keep denying everything, you will never accept that possibility as a present phenomenon.

495
00:35:20,493 --> 00:35:21,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the key.

496
00:35:21,734 --> 00:35:25,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Accepting possibilities as a present negative phenomenon in your experience.

497
00:35:26,297 --> 00:35:26,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's real.

498
00:35:27,158 --> 00:35:28,178
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility is real.

499
00:35:28,559 --> 00:35:32,702
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how to what extent, but I could find out if I apply myself.

500
00:35:35,403 --> 00:35:37,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to deny anything, really.

501
00:35:38,446 --> 00:35:42,188
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to restrain yourself from what you were told it's unwholesome.

502
00:35:42,208 --> 00:35:44,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Greed averse delusion that you can see that it's unwholesome.

503
00:35:45,430 --> 00:35:48,234
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, you don't need to deny further.

504
00:35:48,434 --> 00:35:49,956
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be like overshooting the mark.

505
00:35:50,597 --> 00:35:52,359
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to understand what's in front of you.

506
00:35:52,379 --> 00:35:53,060
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

507
00:35:53,400 --> 00:35:59,288
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole universe is contained within this less than six foot long body and so on.

508
00:36:00,335 --> 00:36:05,839
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say his body is not real, thus none of the universe is real, thus there is no travel, there is no mother, there is no father, there is no life.

509
00:36:06,260 --> 00:36:09,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there were those views at the time that he quite heavily criticized.

510
00:36:10,483 --> 00:36:18,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, nobody gets killed, there's nothing, it's just elements, just... And actually, modern interpretation of Buddhist anatta, it's actually one of those wrong views back in the day that the Buddha criticized.

511
00:36:19,330 --> 00:36:23,253
[SPEAKER_00]: When they say everything is comprised of constantly changing electrons, so nothing is real or nothing

512
00:36:23,713 --> 00:36:24,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing can be mine.

513
00:36:24,854 --> 00:36:25,635
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no self.

514
00:36:26,415 --> 00:36:29,497
[SPEAKER_00]: And people believe that, whereby they said, oh, nobody gets killed.

515
00:36:29,578 --> 00:36:34,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you go and cut everybody on the left side of Ganges with a sharp sword, nobody gets killed.

516
00:36:34,701 --> 00:36:40,846
[SPEAKER_00]: All that happens is the... the... the... like the sword runs through the elements, runs through the electrons and atoms.

517
00:36:40,906 --> 00:36:42,327
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just call it whatever you want.

518
00:36:42,587 --> 00:36:43,548
[SPEAKER_00]: The view is the same.

519
00:36:44,629 --> 00:36:45,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And we'll say it's completely wrong view.

520
00:36:46,781 --> 00:36:51,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're blaming the five aggregates for your assumption, for your sense of self.

521
00:36:51,464 --> 00:36:55,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that it's desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates, that the sense of self is there.

522
00:36:56,048 --> 00:36:57,469
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not the five aggregates.

523
00:36:57,849 --> 00:37:00,431
[SPEAKER_00]: So you killing the five aggregates does not free you from the sense of self.

524
00:37:00,751 --> 00:37:07,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, person who is not freed from the sense of self, when the five aggregates disintegrate, as in when they die, they're still there.

525
00:37:09,225 --> 00:37:10,745
[SPEAKER_00]: They get reborn.

526
00:37:10,985 --> 00:37:16,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds me of when someone's got a problem and it's not, you know, complaining about trying to talk it out.

527
00:37:17,666 --> 00:37:19,407
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, I don't know the answer.

528
00:37:19,467 --> 00:37:21,947
[SPEAKER_01]: But anyway, anyway, everything's impermanent.

529
00:37:21,987 --> 00:37:24,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anyway, it's OK.

530
00:37:24,068 --> 00:37:24,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like this.

531
00:37:25,568 --> 00:37:25,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well.

532
00:37:26,748 --> 00:37:29,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I've got this problem, but I'll just deny it now.

533
00:37:30,089 --> 00:37:38,050
[SPEAKER_00]: No, unfortunately, it's prevalent as a view, but it's just rooted in lazy self-reflection.

534
00:37:39,293 --> 00:37:41,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in self-reflection, it doesn't go far enough.

535
00:37:44,758 --> 00:37:50,422
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you just put a cap on it by saying, ah, well, it's all impermanent anyway, so I won't bother with it.

536
00:37:52,544 --> 00:37:56,247
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just, you know, watch my breath and be peaceful and live a simple life.

537
00:37:56,928 --> 00:38:03,753
[SPEAKER_00]: You can live life as as simple as you want, as a vegetable, and still remain equally ignorant.

538
00:38:04,934 --> 00:38:05,775
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

539
00:38:06,195 --> 00:38:08,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not how complex your life is.

540
00:38:08,600 --> 00:38:14,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you want to simplify it, because it can be too much to start with, to practice.

541
00:38:15,063 --> 00:38:16,383
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's secondary.

542
00:38:16,883 --> 00:38:20,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just creating the suitable condition for you to understand what's right in front of you.

543
00:38:21,585 --> 00:38:35,391
[SPEAKER_00]: But thinking that the goal of your life is to simplify it, or to just do this, just do that, without making the effort to understand it, and go through that unpleasantness of unraveling the deep existential feelings of fear and anxiety.

544
00:38:35,611 --> 00:38:35,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

545
00:38:36,211 --> 00:38:37,332
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no practice without that.

